1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,971
Ninety-three million miles
away, our star is restless.

2
00:00:10,051 --> 00:00:16,072
Beneath its glowing surface, twisted
magnetic fields store unimaginable energy.

3
00:00:16,152 --> 00:00:19,572
And when those fields
finally snap, the Sun hurls

4
00:00:19,652 --> 00:00:23,379
billions of tons of
superheated gas into space.

5
00:00:23,459 --> 00:00:25,667
A solar storm is born.

6
00:00:25,817 --> 00:00:29,846
Within days, it can reach Earth,
painting our skies with light,

7
00:00:29,926 --> 00:00:33,883
and testing the technology
our modern world depends on.

8
00:00:33,963 --> 00:00:37,057
This is the story of those
storms, of how they form,

9
00:00:37,137 --> 00:00:41,095
how they travel, and how we
have learned to watch them.

10
00:00:43,445 --> 00:00:46,027
The Sun holds more than
ninety-nine percent

11
00:00:46,107 --> 00:00:48,706
of all the mass in our solar system.

12
00:00:48,786 --> 00:00:53,222
Deep in its core, at fifteen
million degrees Celsius, hydrogen

13
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:58,115
fuses into helium, releasing
the energy that lights our days.

14
00:00:58,195 --> 00:01:03,493
That energy takes many thousands of
years to work its way out to the surface.

15
00:01:03,573 --> 00:01:08,336
Then, as light, it crosses the gap
to Earth in just over eight minutes.

16
00:01:08,416 --> 00:01:13,190
Its visible surface glows at about
five and a half thousand degrees.

17
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:16,500
Yet above it, the thin
outer atmosphere called the

18
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:20,459
corona is far hotter, at
more than a million degrees.

19
00:01:20,539 --> 00:01:23,126
Exactly how the corona is heated remains

20
00:01:23,206 --> 00:01:26,511
one of the great mysteries
of solar science.

21
00:01:26,591 --> 00:01:29,438
But the Sun is not a calm, solid ball.

22
00:01:29,518 --> 00:01:32,518
It is made of plasma,
gas so hot that its atoms

23
00:01:32,598 --> 00:01:35,852
have been torn apart
into charged particles.

24
00:01:35,932 --> 00:01:39,875
And charged particles in
motion create magnetic fields.

25
00:01:39,955 --> 00:01:44,562
The Sun also spins faster at
its equator than near its poles.

26
00:01:44,642 --> 00:01:48,922
Over time, this uneven rotation
stretches and twists the magnetic

27
00:01:49,002 --> 00:01:53,195
field lines, like rubber bands
wound tighter and tighter.

28
00:01:53,275 --> 00:01:58,832
Where these tangled fields push up through
the surface, they leave a mark we can see.

29
00:02:00,682 --> 00:02:04,342
Sunspots look like dark
blemishes on the face of the Sun.

30
00:02:04,422 --> 00:02:06,744
They are regions where intense magnetic

31
00:02:06,824 --> 00:02:09,966
fields hold back the
flow of heat from below.

32
00:02:10,046 --> 00:02:14,966
At around four thousand degrees, they are
cooler than their surroundings, so they

33
00:02:15,046 --> 00:02:17,374
appear dark, yet they would still blaze

34
00:02:17,454 --> 00:02:20,725
brighter than a full moon
if seen on their own.

35
00:02:20,805 --> 00:02:24,314
Many sunspots are larger
than the entire Earth.

36
00:02:24,394 --> 00:02:27,187
Their numbers rise and
fall in a rhythm called

37
00:02:27,267 --> 00:02:30,636
the solar cycle, which
lasts about eleven years.

38
00:02:30,716 --> 00:02:35,020
At solar minimum, the Sun can
go weeks without a single spot.

39
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:38,201
At solar maximum, it can
be covered with them,

40
00:02:38,281 --> 00:02:42,662
and its entire magnetic field
flips, north becoming south.

41
00:02:42,742 --> 00:02:45,882
In 2024, scientists
announced that the Sun had

42
00:02:45,962 --> 00:02:49,623
entered the maximum phase
of its current cycle.

43
00:02:49,703 --> 00:02:55,337
And the more active the Sun becomes, the
more often its magnetic fields erupt.

44
00:02:57,187 --> 00:03:01,163
When twisted magnetic field lines
suddenly snap and reconnect,

45
00:03:01,243 --> 00:03:04,687
they release their stored
energy in an instant.

46
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:08,205
The result is a solar
flare, an intense flash of

47
00:03:08,285 --> 00:03:12,612
light across the spectrum,
from radio waves to X-rays.

48
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:15,711
A large flare can
release as much energy as

49
00:03:15,791 --> 00:03:19,403
millions of hydrogen
bombs exploding at once.

50
00:03:19,483 --> 00:03:22,058
Scientists rank flares by their strength,

51
00:03:22,138 --> 00:03:25,550
with the most powerful
known as X-class flares.

52
00:03:25,630 --> 00:03:30,522
The largest flare ever measured
erupted in November 2003.

53
00:03:30,602 --> 00:03:35,437
It was so intense that it overwhelmed
the instruments built to record it.

54
00:03:35,517 --> 00:03:40,365
Because a flare is light, its radiation
reaches Earth in about eight minutes,

55
00:03:40,445 --> 00:03:45,935
and it can disrupt radio communication
on the side of the planet facing the Sun.

56
00:03:46,015 --> 00:03:49,075
Often, a flare comes with
something even bigger.

57
00:03:49,155 --> 00:03:53,958
Above the surface, magnetic fields
can also lift enormous arches of

58
00:03:54,038 --> 00:03:59,438
glowing plasma, called prominences,
that hang in the corona for days.

59
00:03:59,518 --> 00:04:04,152
When their magnetic support breaks,
they too can be flung out into space.

60
00:04:04,232 --> 00:04:09,729
A coronal mass ejection, or CME, is
a vast cloud of plasma and magnetic

61
00:04:09,809 --> 00:04:15,127
field blasted away from the Sun's
outer atmosphere, the corona.

62
00:04:15,207 --> 00:04:19,472
A single eruption can carry
a billion tons of material.

63
00:04:19,552 --> 00:04:24,400
The fastest race outward at around
three thousand kilometers per second.

64
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:29,276
As it expands, a CME can grow
larger than the Sun itself.

65
00:04:29,356 --> 00:04:33,069
Most of these eruptions head
off harmlessly into space.

66
00:04:33,149 --> 00:04:36,281
But sometimes, one is
aimed straight at us.

67
00:04:38,631 --> 00:04:41,845
Even on quiet days, the
Sun constantly breathes out

68
00:04:41,925 --> 00:04:45,653
a stream of charged particles
called the solar wind.

69
00:04:45,733 --> 00:04:49,342
It flows past Earth at nearly
a million miles per hour.

70
00:04:49,422 --> 00:04:52,326
A coronal mass ejection
is like a gust inside

71
00:04:52,406 --> 00:04:55,894
that wind, a shock front
plowing through space.

72
00:04:55,974 --> 00:05:00,331
Depending on its speed, it can
cross the hundred and fifty million

73
00:05:00,411 --> 00:05:05,623
kilometers to Earth in as little as
fifteen hours, or take several days.

74
00:05:05,703 --> 00:05:08,644
Fortunately, our planet has a shield.

75
00:05:08,794 --> 00:05:13,253
Earth's molten iron core generates
a magnetic field that surrounds

76
00:05:13,333 --> 00:05:17,622
the planet, a protective bubble
called the magnetosphere.

77
00:05:17,702 --> 00:05:21,712
Most of the time, it deflects
the solar wind around us.

78
00:05:21,792 --> 00:05:27,099
But when a powerful CME strikes, the
magnetosphere is squeezed and shaken.

79
00:05:27,179 --> 00:05:32,489
If the magnetic field of the cloud points
in the opposite direction to Earth's own,

80
00:05:32,569 --> 00:05:37,356
the two can link up, and energy
pours into our magnetic environment.

81
00:05:37,436 --> 00:05:39,982
This is a geomagnetic storm.

82
00:05:40,132 --> 00:05:43,663
On the night side of Earth,
the magnetosphere is stretched

83
00:05:43,743 --> 00:05:47,849
into a long tail, reaching far
beyond the orbit of the Moon.

84
00:05:47,929 --> 00:05:51,484
During a storm, that tail
stores energy, then suddenly

85
00:05:51,564 --> 00:05:55,395
snaps back, hurling
particles toward the planet.

86
00:05:57,245 --> 00:06:01,167
The most beautiful sign of
a solar storm is the aurora.

87
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:04,522
Charged particles guided
by Earth's magnetic field

88
00:06:04,602 --> 00:06:08,395
rain down into the upper
atmosphere near the poles.

89
00:06:08,475 --> 00:06:12,757
There, they collide with oxygen
and nitrogen, making them glow.

90
00:06:12,837 --> 00:06:18,262
Oxygen shines green and red, while
nitrogen adds shades of blue and purple.

91
00:06:18,342 --> 00:06:22,896
Auroras appear around both poles at
the same time, the northern lights

92
00:06:22,976 --> 00:06:27,913
and the southern lights, mirror
images on opposite ends of the world.

93
00:06:27,993 --> 00:06:31,266
Seen from orbit, auroras
ripple across the planet

94
00:06:31,346 --> 00:06:35,138
like living curtains,
hundreds of kilometers tall.

95
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:39,686
During strong storms, they spread
far beyond the polar regions.

96
00:06:39,766 --> 00:06:44,140
In May 2024, the strongest
geomagnetic storm in more than

97
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:49,357
twenty years lit up skies as
far south as Florida and Mexico.

98
00:06:49,437 --> 00:06:53,112
Millions of people saw an
aurora for the very first time.

99
00:06:53,192 --> 00:06:58,368
But the same storms that create such
beauty can also cause real damage.

100
00:07:00,718 --> 00:07:03,298
On September 1st, 1859, the English

101
00:07:03,378 --> 00:07:06,565
astronomer Richard
Carrington was sketching

102
00:07:06,645 --> 00:07:12,791
sunspots when he saw two brilliant beads
of white light flare up on the Sun.

103
00:07:12,871 --> 00:07:15,783
It was the first solar
flare ever recorded.

104
00:07:15,863 --> 00:07:18,995
Less than a day later, the
eruption reached Earth.

105
00:07:19,075 --> 00:07:22,375
Auroras glowed over the
Caribbean, so bright that people

106
00:07:22,455 --> 00:07:25,842
in some places could read
by their light at night.

107
00:07:25,922 --> 00:07:30,003
Telegraph systems across Europe
and North America failed.

108
00:07:30,083 --> 00:07:34,810
Some operators received electric
shocks, and some lines kept working even

109
00:07:34,890 --> 00:07:39,803
after their batteries were disconnected,
powered by the storm itself.

110
00:07:39,883 --> 00:07:44,488
In 1859, the world had
little technology to lose.

111
00:07:44,568 --> 00:07:47,033
Today, it is very different.

112
00:07:47,183 --> 00:07:49,808
In March 1989, a geomagnetic storm

113
00:07:49,888 --> 00:07:53,228
collapsed the power
grid in Quebec, Canada,

114
00:07:53,308 --> 00:07:56,728
in about ninety seconds,
leaving six million

115
00:07:56,808 --> 00:08:00,519
people without electricity for nine hours.

116
00:08:00,599 --> 00:08:05,906
In 2022, even a minor storm warmed
and expanded Earth's upper atmosphere,

117
00:08:05,986 --> 00:08:10,636
dragging dozens of newly
launched satellites out of orbit.

118
00:08:10,716 --> 00:08:15,882
Strong storms can disturb GPS
navigation, satellite communications,

119
00:08:15,962 --> 00:08:20,952
and the radio links used by
aircraft flying over the poles.

120
00:08:21,032 --> 00:08:23,321
Astronauts are at risk too.

121
00:08:23,471 --> 00:08:29,201
Outside the protection of Earth's magnetic
field, on the way to the Moon or Mars,

122
00:08:29,281 --> 00:08:32,214
a burst of solar particles could deliver a

123
00:08:32,294 --> 00:08:36,179
dangerous dose of radiation
in a matter of hours.

124
00:08:36,259 --> 00:08:39,186
And in July 2012, an
eruption as powerful as

125
00:08:39,266 --> 00:08:42,917
the Carrington event tore
through Earth's orbit.

126
00:08:42,997 --> 00:08:45,632
We missed it by about nine days.

127
00:08:47,982 --> 00:08:52,837
That is why a fleet of spacecraft
now watches the Sun around the clock.

128
00:08:52,917 --> 00:08:57,005
NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory
captures the Sun in many

129
00:08:57,085 --> 00:09:02,127
wavelengths of light, recording
a new image every twelve seconds.

130
00:09:02,207 --> 00:09:05,322
The joint European and
American observatory

131
00:09:05,402 --> 00:09:07,848
SOHO has been watching since 1995,

132
00:09:07,928 --> 00:09:11,192
blocking the bright disk
of the Sun to reveal

133
00:09:11,272 --> 00:09:14,540
eruptions streaming through the corona.

134
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:18,666
And NASA's Parker Solar Probe
has flown closer to the Sun than

135
00:09:18,746 --> 00:09:23,176
any spacecraft in history,
passing through the corona itself.

136
00:09:23,256 --> 00:09:27,689
At its closest, it races past
at around seven hundred thousand

137
00:09:27,769 --> 00:09:32,578
kilometers per hour, the fastest
object ever built by humans.

138
00:09:32,658 --> 00:09:38,187
The European Space Agency's Solar
Orbiter, built in partnership with NASA,

139
00:09:38,267 --> 00:09:41,521
has climbed out of the
plane of the planets,

140
00:09:41,601 --> 00:09:46,745
and in 2025 it gave us our first
ever views of the Sun's poles.

141
00:09:46,825 --> 00:09:50,896
The poles are where the Sun's
magnetic cycle may be decided.

142
00:09:50,976 --> 00:09:55,939
Closer to home, spacecraft parked
about one and a half million kilometers

143
00:09:56,019 --> 00:10:01,708
from Earth, between us and the Sun,
measure the solar wind before it arrives.

144
00:10:01,788 --> 00:10:04,650
They give forecasters
a warning of up to an

145
00:10:04,730 --> 00:10:08,384
hour before a storm's
magnetic cloud reaches us.

146
00:10:08,464 --> 00:10:11,502
That is enough time for
power companies to protect

147
00:10:11,582 --> 00:10:15,132
their grids and for satellite
operators to prepare.

148
00:10:15,212 --> 00:10:18,592
Space weather forecasters rate geomagnetic

149
00:10:18,672 --> 00:10:23,079
storms on a scale from
G1, minor, to G5, extreme.

150
00:10:23,159 --> 00:10:26,651
Like weather forecasts on
Earth, their predictions are

151
00:10:26,731 --> 00:10:31,035
improving every year, but the
Sun can still surprise us.

152
00:10:32,885 --> 00:10:36,311
The Sun gives us light,
warmth, and life itself.

153
00:10:36,391 --> 00:10:42,434
But it is also a dynamic, violent star,
and we live inside its outer atmosphere.

154
00:10:42,514 --> 00:10:48,098
Every aurora that dances across the night
sky is a reminder of that connection.

155
00:10:48,178 --> 00:10:52,204
The next great solar storm is
not a question of if, but when.

156
00:10:52,284 --> 00:10:54,782
And thanks to the
spacecraft that keep watch

157
00:10:54,862 --> 00:10:58,039
on our restless star,
we will see it coming.
